findings.detail:
In the latched pattern, each tick _apply_velocity() makes last tick's _safe_velocity the body's sim_velocity. Through ATTACK and the 21-frame RECOVER hold, _stop() zeroes _nav.velocity but leaves _safe_velocity, and the avoidance's own internal velocity, as they were. The first walking tick of the back-off therefore applies an old velocity: here 4.1 m/s toward the player, while the desired velocity points away. The avoidance then keeps the body on that course for several frames. Seen in first person:
- a Punk that has just attacked lunges past the player's shoulder and orbits him at 0.76-1.1 m instead of stepping back;
- that orbit sweeps through the lens and doubles up with another Punk in front (the two-Punk wall in finding 2);
- in other episodes a Punk zips sideways at 4-6 m/s, faster than its 2.6 m/s walk.
This breaks rubric Q7 and A3.1's 'RECOVER backs off ... walks back out to its slot'. It is a mechanical cause behind P1-int call 17's 'walks the long way round', which was reported but not traced.

R8 misses in 4 of 5 six-Punk acts, p95 50-62%. Attributing each covered lens cell to its enemy shows what causes it:
- 73-86% of the coverage in over-half samples comes from Punks not in WINDUP or ATTACK;
- in observed and passive, the RECOVER hold contributes 44-52% (standing at strike range for recover_hold_s) and token holders in ENGAGE waiting for the director's go another 18-28%;
- in fight and Waves, a Punk walking back after a hit (APPROACH) contributes 54-58%;
- a winding-up Punk contributes only 4-25%; alone it covers p50 34% of the lens.
The RECOVER hold and the waiting holder stack in the front wedge while rear attackers hit. Rear hits' knockback also shoves the player into a waiting Punk's guard: point-blank frames (a Punk under 0.8 m in front) are 1-7% of samples, with mean coverage 0.58-0.67, mostly while the player is STAGGERed. In first person this reads as a red wall and fists at the lens, with no attack to read, exactly when two attacks land from behind.

